1
00:00:00,831 --> 00:00:10,723
Welcome to Japan's prefectures, part 4 of 4. First, I clear the chosen names
for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:11,387 --> 00:00:21,325
We work through 17 of the Japan regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:21,984 --> 00:00:24,027
Let's bring Tottori into view.

4
00:00:27,890 --> 00:00:40,765
Tottori faces the Sea of Japan on the west of Honshū, with Shimane and Okayama
around it and Hyōgo to the south, and the city of Tottori on the shore.

5
00:00:41,425 --> 00:00:54,300
It is the least populated prefecture, with about 540,000 people, and in 2015
it became the last in Japan to get a Starbucks, joking that it had the sand
dunes instead.

6
00:00:54,952 --> 00:01:03,137
Remember the sixteen kilometres of sand dunes with camels for the tourists,
and the waterfowl that once paid the tax.

7
00:01:03,823 --> 00:01:05,692
Let's bring Shimane into view.

8
00:01:10,922 --> 00:01:25,214
Shimane is on the Sea of Japan coast at the far west of Honshū, with Tottori
above it and Yamaguchi and Hiroshima to the south, and the city of Matsue on
the shore of Lake Shinji.

9
00:01:25,879 --> 00:01:37,396
The Iwami silver mine here supplied perhaps a third of the world’s silver in
the 1600s and is now a UNESCO world heritage site.

10
00:01:38,058 --> 00:01:44,618
Remember the oldest shrine in Japan at Izumo, where all the gods of the
country gather each October.

11
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:48,574
Let's bring Okayama into view.

12
00:01:51,506 --> 00:02:02,164
Okayama faces the Inland Sea on the west of Honshū, with Tottori and Hyōgo
above and Hiroshima below, and the city of Okayama on the bay.

13
00:02:02,817 --> 00:02:12,500
The Kōrakuen garden here is the third of Japan’s three great gardens, and the
prefecture claims the story of Momotarō, the peach boy.

14
00:02:13,150 --> 00:02:19,245
Remember the peach boy, and the white peaches and muscat grapes that back the
story.

15
00:02:19,931 --> 00:02:22,358
Let's bring Hiroshima into view.

16
00:02:25,909 --> 00:02:35,371
Hiroshima faces the Inland Sea on the west of Honshū, with the city of
Hiroshima built across the delta of the Ōta river.

17
00:02:36,034 --> 00:02:49,165
The first atomic bomb was dropped here on 6 August 1945, and the Genbaku Dome
was left standing as it was and is now a UNESCO world heritage site.

18
00:02:49,818 --> 00:02:57,829
Remember the wide island of the river delta, and the torii gate that seems to
float at high tide out at Miyajima.

19
00:02:58,599 --> 00:03:00,596
Let's bring Yamaguchi into view.

20
00:03:10,263 --> 00:03:21,943
Yamaguchi sits at the far west end of Honshū, facing the Sea of Japan to the
north and the Inland Sea to the south, with the city of Shimonoseki on its
western tip.

21
00:03:22,603 --> 00:03:33,563
Its Chōshū domain led the overthrow of the shogunate in 1868, and eight of
Japan’s prime ministers have come from the prefecture.

22
00:03:34,233 --> 00:03:41,141
Remember the mouth of the mountain, and the karst plateau with ten kilometres
of cave under it.

23
00:03:41,898 --> 00:03:43,941
Let's bring Kagawa into view.

24
00:03:52,634 --> 00:04:04,279
Kagawa is the smallest prefecture in Japan, in the north of Shikoku, with the
city of Takamatsu on the Seto Inland Sea and Ehime and Tokushima around it.

25
00:04:04,941 --> 00:04:13,207
It eats the most udon of any prefecture, with hundreds of shops in its capital
serving Sanuki udon.

26
00:04:13,875 --> 00:04:21,805
Remember the fragrant river, the smallest prefecture, and the bowls of udon on
the counter of every shop in Takamatsu.

27
00:04:22,583 --> 00:04:25,393
Let's bring Tokushima into view.

28
00:04:33,636 --> 00:04:44,770
Tokushima is on the Pacific in the east of Shikoku, across the strait from
Kōchi and between Kagawa and Ehime, with the city of Tokushima on the delta.

29
00:04:45,436 --> 00:04:57,545
The Naruto whirlpools in the strait off it are among the largest in the world,
up to 20 metres across, and the Awa Odori festival draws over a million people
every August.

30
00:04:58,209 --> 00:05:05,419
Remember the virtue island and the whirlpools, and the August dance that
empties the streets of Tokushima.

31
00:05:06,107 --> 00:05:09,219
Let's bring Nagasaki into view.

32
00:05:12,144 --> 00:05:21,954
Nagasaki lies in the north-west of Kyūshū on the East China Sea, with Saga to
its east and the city of Nagasaki curled around its harbour.

33
00:05:22,614 --> 00:05:34,212
For more than two hundred years the artificial island of Dejima was the only
place in Japan where foreigners could trade, and Dutch books were the only
window on the West.

34
00:05:34,868 --> 00:05:41,985
Remember the long cape with the second atomic bomb, and the small fan-shaped
island where Dutch learning came in.

35
00:05:42,656 --> 00:05:45,083
Let's bring Ehime into view.

36
00:05:48,441 --> 00:05:59,145
Ehime is in the west of Shikoku, facing both the Pacific and the Seto Inland
Sea, with the city of Matsuyama on the bay and Tokushima to its east.

37
00:05:59,806 --> 00:06:12,136
Dōgo Onsen here claims to be Japan’s oldest hot spring, mentioned in the
eighth century, and it inspired the bathhouse in the animated film Spirited
Away.

38
00:06:12,788 --> 00:06:19,998
Remember the lovely princess and the oldest hot spring in the country, with
the keep of Matsuyama castle above it.

39
00:06:20,672 --> 00:06:23,482
Let's bring Fukuoka into view.

40
00:06:26,543 --> 00:06:38,397
Fukuoka is on the north coast of Kyūshū on the Sea of Japan side, with the
city of Fukuoka on its wide bay, Saga to the west and Ōita and Kumamoto around
the rest.

41
00:06:39,059 --> 00:06:50,356
It is closer to Seoul than to Tokyo, and it has been Japan’s gate to the
continent since the Mongol invasions failed on its coast in 1274 and 1281.

42
00:06:51,020 --> 00:06:57,673
Remember the fortune hill, the ramen invented here, and the food stalls that
fill the streets at night.

43
00:06:58,351 --> 00:07:00,220
Let's bring Kōchi into view.

44
00:07:04,392 --> 00:07:15,816
Kōchi is on the Pacific in the south of Shikoku, the one prefecture of the
island that never reaches the Seto Inland Sea, with the city of Kōchi at the
head of a deep bay.

45
00:07:16,468 --> 00:07:27,509
The Shimanto river here is called Japan’s last clear stream, with no dam on
its main course, and the prefecture is the wettest in the country.

46
00:07:28,332 --> 00:07:34,938
Remember the high knowledge and Ryoma on every souvenir, and the clear river
with no dam on it.

47
00:07:35,832 --> 00:07:38,050
Let's bring Saga into view.

48
00:07:44,192 --> 00:07:53,143
Saga lies in the north-west of Kyūshū, facing the Sea of Japan between Fukuoka
and Nagasaki, with the city of Saga inland on the plain.

49
00:07:53,822 --> 00:08:05,629
Arita in it made Japan’s first porcelain around 1616, after a Korean potter
found kaolin, and it was shipped to Europe from Imari.

50
00:08:06,330 --> 00:08:12,553
Remember the moated settlement at Yoshinogari, and the blue and white
porcelain that crossed to Europe.

51
00:08:13,279 --> 00:08:15,067
Let's bring Ōita into view.

52
00:08:22,090 --> 00:08:33,642
Ōita is on the east of Kyūshū, facing the Pacific and the Bungo Channel, with
the city of Ōita on the coast, Kumamoto to the west and Miyazaki to the south.

53
00:08:34,296 --> 00:08:41,332
It produces more hot spring water than any other prefecture, and Beppu alone
has over 2,000 vents.

54
00:08:41,991 --> 00:08:48,342
Remember the great field of many rice paddies, and the boiling pits of Beppu
on the coast.

55
00:08:49,039 --> 00:08:51,512
Let's bring Kumamoto into view.

56
00:08:58,286 --> 00:09:10,386
Kumamoto is in the west of Kyūshū, facing the East China Sea, with the city of
Kumamoto on its delta, Fukuoka above it and Ōita to the east.

57
00:09:11,039 --> 00:09:19,955
Mount Aso here has one of the largest volcanic calderas in the world, 25
kilometres across, with towns and farms inside it.

58
00:09:20,613 --> 00:09:26,453
Remember the bear that is only a spelling accident, and the villages that live
inside the crater.

59
00:09:27,248 --> 00:09:29,245
Let's bring Miyazaki into view.

60
00:09:36,523 --> 00:09:46,113
Miyazaki is on the Pacific in the south-east of Kyūshū, between Ōita and
Kagoshima, with the city of Miyazaki on the coast.

61
00:09:46,821 --> 00:09:53,555
It has the most sunshine hours of any prefecture and grows mangoes sold as
eggs of the sun.

62
00:09:54,206 --> 00:10:01,416
Remember the shrine cape and the gorge where the sun goddess hid, under the
sunniest sky in Japan.

63
00:10:02,226 --> 00:10:04,734
Let's bring Kagoshima into view.

64
00:10:10,716 --> 00:10:22,999
Kagoshima is the southern tip of Kyūshū, facing the Pacific, with the city of
Kagoshima under a volcano and Kumamoto and Miyazaki above it.

65
00:10:23,692 --> 00:10:33,711
Sakurajima in that bay is one of the world’s most active volcanoes and dusts
the city with ash most weeks, and the prefecture grows most of Japan’s sweet
potatoes.

66
00:10:34,365 --> 00:10:41,319
Remember the deer child island with its smoking volcano, and the rockets
launched from the island to the south of it.

67
00:10:42,036 --> 00:10:44,637
Let's bring Okinawa into view.

68
00:10:50,088 --> 00:11:03,440
Okinawa is Japan’s southernmost prefecture, a chain of islands in the East
China Sea several hundred kilometres to the south-west of Kyūshū, with the
city of Naha on the main island.

69
00:11:04,092 --> 00:11:13,008
It was the independent Ryūkyū Kingdom, trading with China and south-east Asia,
until Japan annexed it in 1879.

70
00:11:13,668 --> 00:11:21,772
Remember the rope in the offing, the island whose name is not Japanese, and
the people with among the longest lives in the world.

71
00:11:29,793 --> 00:11:54,921
17 regions of Japan, one for each target of the map: Tottori, Shimane,
Okayama, Hiroshima, Yamaguchi, Kagawa, Tokushima, Nagasaki, Ehime, Fukuoka,
Kōchi, Saga, Ōita, Kumamoto, Miyazaki, Kagoshima and Okinawa.

72
00:11:55,585 --> 00:12:05,779
Remember the shape of Japan from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
